r league needs to step  in yeah without a doubt i mean the  premier league sits on top of a pyramid  right and it's a really proud thing we  should have in our game this pyramid of  the system that goes all the way down  into the non  you know non-league as well and the  pyramid sits on the top of that  and benefits completely from him so you  can go through players you see holly  watkins got a hat-trick at the weekend  he was scoring against kosher just a few  seasons ago when he was at  exit harry maguire the captain of  england i remember him at sheffield  united  so playing against colchester so and you  can probably go through most of the  england team most of the premiership  teams  and all their players have come from  grassroots  they've all played under nines under  10's football somewhere so yeah the  premier league  seems to think that we suck from it but  it's not the case  it sucks from the rest of the  um pyramid system so yeah it really  should step in and do something  are you optimistic they will they have  to i mean if they can't see it for  themselves i mean  if they think they're enjoying this  penthouse for you and they're not in  danger from the building that's  crumbling below them  they're mad they're absolutely mad how  important  are clubs like colchester to the local  community  i think it's massively important not  just to not just to the people that  support colchester united like i say  people come here and give blood people  come here and you know  get married people come here and have  weights people  come here for all sorts of reasons it is  a community stadium as i said more  people come here for non-football events  than they do  and you know it's a hub the very first  thing we did when they did lockdown  was we decided we wanted to be part of a  solution so we got in touch with all the  authorities and said look we've got a  stadium  if you want to come and use it if the  nhs want to use it in any way  do so we don't want any money for it and  we're not the only club we're not saints  every football club that i know has  acted in that way has done something to  be a part of the solution for the  pandemic we've got the covered tasks  around the corner we've got a cover test  going on on here now yeah and i say we  don't charge anything for that  we just want it to be part of a solution  and as i say that i think football's  stepped in our community boys have been  brilliant delivering parcels  our players have been ringing pensioners  been bringing the vulnerable  so i feel we've done our part but we  need a bit of help too  to give a sense of scale macclesfield  town recently went under  with debts that amounted to gareth  bale's reported weekly wage  but premier league clubs may feel that  too much is being asked of them  millions of pounds already flow from the  top division to lower leagues every year  and they too are affected by the ban on  supporters  in an interview this week the premier  league's chief executive richard masters  said  clubs feel they've been hit with a  quadruple whammy firstly that the  optimism of october the first has been  taken away  secondly that there will be a sports  bailout but it won't include football  thirdly that the premier league will be  expected to secure the future of the efl  while dealing with the implications of  having no fans until possibly march  and finally the opening up of  entertainment arenas within sometimes a  couple of miles of football grounds  without any road map for the return of  football supporters  the culture secretary oliver downdon  told sophie ridge on sunday two weeks  ago  that the premier league needs to play  its part and help smaller clubs  he's vowed to throw everything at  getting fans back in stadiums  sooner rather than later but critics say  the government needs to do more  firstly get the test trace and isolate  system working  properly that's affecting absolutely  every aspect of our life  and it affects our ability as football  supporters  to get back into stadia secondly get  football clubs particularly those  in league one on and league two and in  the non-league you know get them round  the table  or albeit on zoom at the moment and talk  them through  the logic of what you're doing and how  you think we can put together a plan for  football i don't think there's a social  problem that britain has  that can't be helped by sport in general  and football specifically so if  anybody in government is snobbish about  football i i don't know why they would  be and i think it's time they should  grow up because actually football is  part of the solution  in westminster you hear an awful lot of  people worrying understandably about the  future of the arts and the impact that  kobud could have  on our culture but for millions of  people coming to grounds like this  supporting their local team is the  highlight of their week  and for lower league clubs in particular  they're really on the brink  there are hopes that a bailout deal  could be agreed later this month after  the transfer window shuts  without it dozens of clubs will be  fighting for their future
 

